{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>These are the names of the men who shall apportion the land to you.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: 'shall apportion to you' — on your behalf, for each and every prince was a trustee for his tribe, and he would distribute the tribe's inheritance among the families and the men, selecting a fine portion for each individual; and whatever they do is considered as though their agents did it. And he mentioned Eleazar first, because Joshua went forth at his word. And he mentioned ten tribes with a prince for each and every one, and did not mention Reuben and Gad, for they had already taken their portion across the Jordan eastward. And he began with the princes of Judah, just as the lot came out first. And it says: ולמטה בני דן נשיא בקי בן יגלי." "<b>And you should be roused to ask:</b> since they were all princes — one prince per tribe, tribe by tribe — why did the text not mention the word נשיא until Dan and onward? And it is possible to say: he did not need to mention 'prince' regarding Caleb, because he was well-known from the spies, of whom it is written there (Numbers 13:2) ״כל נשיא בהם״; and regarding Simeon, because Zimri son of Salu was their prince, therefore the tribe of Simeon did not merit having a prince named over it, even after Zimri's death; and regarding Benjamin he did not mention it either, because of the concubine at Gibeah. And they said in the Midrash Tanchuma, in the portion of Beha'alotekha: אלידד בן כסלון — this is Eldad, the companion of Medad, who were prophets; and since he was a prophet, the verse did not need to call him נשיא."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:56.070388821Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}